The following is actually a pretty old idea, but it was never taken up again
(as far as I know). Since I just read Derek's how-to on YANIs, I'll try to
present one. My sister coded most of the following back in the day and we
asked here for help (and the devteam, too, with no reply :). Since both of
us are now Crawl developers, to us this YANI is moot.

There is a fundamental break in consistency when you reach the dungeon level
containing the magic portal to the Quest. I have forgotten the precise
statement, but it did convey a sense of urgency. This is completely wrong:
you can take as much time as you like (and for certain classes, you should
actually wait with tackling Home:1 until you are buff enough).

Next, once you enter the portal, there is a dramatic text explaining how it
all went wrong at home and that your help is needed. Again, urgency is
implied (but not meant). Here, the most disturbing feature is that Quest
guards and attackers peacefully walk side by side. This is grotesque.

Okay, both points are pure flavour, and could be rectified with different
messages. However, we felt that it would be much more interesting to
provide some (mild) prize to those who actually take the warnings
seriously. Here is what we coded:

* Using the grudge patch (Pasi Kallinen? - I may be wrong here, and will get
accused for not doing proper research), we made guardians and attackers
fight each other. [1]

* Once you hear the message about the magic portal, a counter starts. It
stops when you talk to the Quest leader.

* A second timer counts how many turns you spend on Home:1 before you reach
the Quest leader.

[Note: it may happen due to the grudge, that the Quest leader dies. We
simply excluded the leader from the grudge but it would have been better to
unblock the stairs in this case. The prize is not available then anyway.]

* We count how many Quest guards lived at the beginning of the operations,
and when talking to the leader.

(* It may be that we also counted the number of killed attackers - I
forgot.)

Out of all these numbers (time used for finding the portal, time spent
reaching the leader, and ratio of surviving guards, and possibly ration of
killed attackers), we calculated a score, ranging between 0 and 6. (I have
forgotten the details, but could perhaps find them on an old computer.) In
true Nethack fashion, there are two prizes, each with the same chance:

Gear: The quest leader improves your worn/wielded equipment, using the
enchant weapon/armour code (so a chosen item may not improve due to high
enchantment). The item is chosen for each step randomly. Unlike the
standard enchantment scrolls, however, gear cannot evaporate. [2]

Portal: The quest leader opens a portal, leading from Home:1 to a level in
the main dungeon. (Suppose that the quest portal is on dungeon level D.
Then the new portal would lead to level D-rnd(score)-1, or similar.)

---

Some remarks:
* There is absolutely no punishment if you skip this Quest game.
* If you take it on, however, there are plenty of means to achieve a good
score: detect traps and magic mapping for quickly finding the portal; all
of jumping, speed, wands, stinking clouds etc. on Home:1. The score
computing formula needs some constants depending on class: the Ranger quest
is much harder to navigate (and hence needs much more generous timers for
high score) than the Wizard's, and so on. We tested our numbers so far that
reaching a score of 6 was possible with all classes, in each case this
being a nontrivial undertaking.
* It obviously repairs the two flavour defects mentioned above. Also, it is
fun to see guards/attackers fight and die.
* [1] The grudge patch makes opposing monsters fight each other when they
are neighboured. This is still not optimal: on Home:1, opponents should
actively move towards their enemies.
* [2] The Gear prize can be used in two ways: you keep all your clothes on,
and can expect an improvement in AC by score, so up to 6. Or you wield/wear
precisely one item, enchanted as high as possible. Then all those (maximal
6) tries will go into this item which cannot break in the process. You will
end up with the occasional +8 weapon this way (or better).
* The Portal prize was probably the least developed aspect of the thing (it
was also the only part not working properly when we abandoned the project -
we had issues with pets and such): it was supposed to create a loop in the
dungeon graph. Besides being fun, it would allow you to bypass several
dungeon levels during the amulet run: the portals are not subject to the
mysterious force.
* We added plenty of speech when you meet the leader, depending on the score
and how many guards perished. This all works fine, except for the Rogue
quest, where the basic idea of the quest leader handing you a prize fails.
We had to stretch things a bit there.

Bottom line: I understand that the vanilla devteam does not care about the
initial two points, and I also understand that Spork is targeting a highly
spoiled audience, so that issues like theme (which usually ia a more newbie
oriented realm) become less important. Thank you for reading this anyway!

David


